 ## Abstract
 
-**Proven.** We separate finite-order representation of a local response from identifiability in a free-fall measurement. An electric-tidal quotient has five representatives through weight four. A nonzero single pole can be interpolated at \(K\) positive frequencies by unrestricted real derivative coefficients of degree \(N\) if and only if \(N\ge2K-1\). For a positive reciprocal relaxation spectrum, two exact calibrated complex samples instead identify one observable relaxation time through a moment-variance equality. Finite precision cannot uniformly separate arbitrarily close poles. **Counterexample candidate.** A damped scalar-charge realization supplies reciprocal forces and a controlled one-pole reduction. **Imported from prior work.** A stored PSR J0337+1715 analysis reports no detection. Retaining all 90 nuisance directions and estimating a specified covariance addresses identified truncation and coverage failures. A separately calibrated six-coefficient confidence region gives 95.17--95.43 percent inclusion on independent simulations in four specified covariance conditions. Corrected physical-drive coefficients and dedicated live transient responses extend the benchmark. Recomputed timing derivatives expose substantial weak-subspace rotation despite small column changes; a weak pulse-count candidate requires an unphysical unconstrained compensation. The result is conditional theory and inference methodology, with empirical-promotion failures retained, rather than a numerical EOS-matched or drive-independent SEP exclusion.
