213: ### 3.5 Observable-pole uniqueness and its finite-precision boundary
214: 
215: **Proven.** Suppose \(H(i\omega)=c_0+\int a(d\tau)/(1+i\omega\tau)\), with real \(c_0\) and a nonnegative finite measure on \(\tau>0\). At two calibrated frequencies \(0<l<h\), let \(S_\omega=-\operatorname{Im}H(i\omega)/\omega\) and define
216: 
217: \begin{equation}
218: R=\frac{h^2S_h-l^2S_l}{h^2-l^2},\qquad
219: D=\frac{\operatorname{Re}H(il)-\operatorname{Re}H(ih)}{h^2-l^2},\qquad
220: Q=\frac{S_l-S_h}{h^2-l^2}.
221: \label{eq:positive-moments}
222: \end{equation}
223: 
224: These are the zeroth, first and second moments of \(d\mu=\tau a(d\tau)/[(1+l^2\tau^2)(1+h^2\tau^2)]\). Therefore
225: 
226: \begin{equation}
227: RQ-D^2=R^2\operatorname{Var}_{\mu/R}(\tau)\ge0.
228: \label{eq:positive-single-pole}
229: \end{equation}
230: 
231: For \(R>0\), equality holds exactly for one observable relaxation time \(\tau_0=D/R\). Its amplitude is \(a_0=R(1+l^2\tau_0^2)(1+h^2\tau_0^2)/\tau_0\), and either real part fixes \(c_0\). Instantaneous mass at zero time is inseparable from \(c_0\). This assumes exact common drive/readout calibration; it is not an observed equality in J0337.
