740: 
741: \subsection{A worked white-dwarf charge: retarded scattering memory and
742: its orbital-timescale
743: collapse}\label{a-worked-white-dwarf-charge-retarded-scattering-memory-and-its-orbital-timescale-collapse}
744: 
745: \textbf{Imported from prior work.} The inner white dwarf of PSR
746: J0337+1715 has timing mass 0.1975 \(M_\odot\) and spectroscopic
747: \(T_{\rm eff}=15{,}800\pm100\) K, \(\log g=5.82\pm0.05\) and
748: \(R=0.091\pm0.005\,R_\odot\) \cite{kaplan2014j0337}. The orbital
749: elements follow
750: \cite{ransom2014triple,archibald2018universality,voisin2020sep}.
751: 
752: \textbf{Counterexample candidate.} We asked whether the smallest
753: internal state of this carrier, the displacement of its own matter, can
754: be the lag-responding state of Section 3. The declared theory has one
755: massless scalar with \(A(\varphi)=e^{\beta\varphi^2/2}\), \(\beta=-4\),
756: \(\alpha=\beta\varphi\), and linearization point
757: \(\varphi_\infty=10^{-3}\). The background is a static, weak-field, hot
758: helium-core model with \(M=0.198\,M_\odot\), \(R=6.91\times10^9\) cm,
759: \(T_{\rm eff}=15{,}898\) K, \(\log g=5.740\) and a gray Eddington
760: envelope built with Rosseland tables. An ingoing C\(^3\) pulse
761: \(U=\eta r_0 g((t+x/c)/D)\), \(g(s)=(4s(1-s))^4\), \(D=1.717\) ms,
762: \(\eta=10^{-30}\), drives the coupled evolution of baryons, gas, photon
763: transport and the returned GR metric until \(T=2D\). The compact charge
764: is \(q=-U_{\rm out}/M\), so the exterior monopole is
765: \(\delta\varphi\to-qGM/(c^2r)\). Interior refinement by 1, 2 and 4,
766: followed by mass normalization, gives
767: 
768: \begin{equation}
769: q(T)=-2.335\times10^{-51},\qquad \frac{q}{\eta\varphi_\infty^2}=-2.335\times10^{-15},
770: \label{eq:wd-charge}
771: \end{equation}
772: 
773: with observed order 2.00 and a 2x-to-4x change of \(-1.2\%\).
774: 
775: \textbf{Counterexample candidate.} The charge is a dynamical response.
776: The instantaneous product of the pulse and the background geometry
777: contributes \(10^{-8}\) of it, while the rest-mass redistribution of
778: baryons contributes 0.999996. The redistribution conserves mass, and
779: 99.9\% of the endpoint charge comes from layers that the pulse has
780: already left. Disabling the Planck--Larkin occupation probabilities in
781: the equation of state and opacities changes the charge by at most
782: \(2.2\times10^{-10}\).
783: 
784: \textbf{Proven.} For pressureless matter at linear order, the radial
785: displacement
786: 
787: \begin{equation}
788: \xi=4c^2\frac{a^2}{B^2}\eta R_dD^2\Big[\Big(\frac{\varphi_0'}{r}-\frac{\varphi_0}{r^2}\Big)F_2(p)+\frac{\varphi_0x'}{rcD}F_1(p)\Big],\qquad p=\frac{t+x/c}{D},
789: \label{eq:free-fall}
790: \end{equation}
791: 
792: with \(F_1=\int_0^pg\) and \(F_2=\int_0^pF_1\), solves
793: \(\ddot\xi=-c^2(a^2/B^2)\partial_r[\alpha(\varphi_0)\delta\varphi]\)
794: exactly (the symbolic residual is zero). Passing the resulting face mass
795: fluxes \(4\pi r^2B\rho_0\xi\) through the same retarded propagator
796: reproduces the continuum limit of the coupled chain to
797: \(5.2\times10^{-5}\).
798: 
799: \textbf{Counterexample candidate.} The charge is linear in the face
800: densities, and its kernel is nearly single-signed: a relative density
801: change of 0.9988 in the maximum norm is needed to reverse the sign. The
802: kernel centroid lies 363 km below the surface, at gray optical depth
803: 1.06, so the charge-carrying layers form the photosphere.
804: 
805: \textbf{Counterexample candidate.} The endpoint value is not a static
806: charge. The static-window monopole of the displaced configuration is
807: \(3.6\times10^{-54}\), which is 1/640 of it. The remainder is the part
808: of the retarded monopole,
809: \(q(u)=M^{-1}\sum_e w_e\,(c/2r_e)\int_{u-r_e/c}^{u+r_e/c}\delta M_e\,dt\)
810: with \(w=\alpha(\varphi_0)a\), that has not yet cancelled inside the
811: light-crossing window. We checked this with a whole-star linear
812: adiabatic radial model with Newtonian self-gravity, driven by the same
813: force and by the regular pulse reflected through the centre. The model
814: reproduces the endpoint charge to \(-0.81\%\) and the free-fall
815: displacement to \(1.2\times10^{-6}\). Over the full history:
816: 
817: \begin{itemize}
818: \tightlist
819: \item
820:   While the pulse moves inward (to 0.35 s), the charge stays negative
821:   and grows by about twelve orders of magnitude, reaching
822:   \(-7.5\times10^{-41}\) when the pulse arrives at the centre.
823: \item
824:   On the outgoing pass, the signal accumulates along the pulse's own
825:   light cone and oscillates in sign. Its largest value,
826:   \(3.5\times10^{-36}\) (\(1.5\times10^{-11}\) of the incident
827:   amplitude), occurs at exit.
828: \item
829:   After the pulse has left, the star rings in its radial p modes, with
830:   dominant periods of 44--59 s and an amplitude of order \(10^{-44}\).
831: \end{itemize}
832: 
833: \textbf{Proven.} Because the radial stiffness is positive definite
834: (\(\omega_0^2=1.25\times10^{-3}\) s\(^{-2}\)), the post-pulse charge is
835: a finite sum of nonzero-frequency modes, and its time average vanishes.
836: For positive mode damping it tends to zero. The permanent charge is
837: therefore zero at first order in \(\eta\).
838: 
839: \textbf{Proven.} The corrections to the endpoint value are small:
840: 
841: \begin{itemize}
842: \tightlist
843: \item
844:   The self-consistent GR fixed point differs from the once-returned
845:   solution by at most \(2\pi G\rho_{\max}T^2=4.5\times10^{-18}\), from
846:   radial self-gravity feedback.
847: \item
848:   Outside a spherical source in flat space, \(U=r\,\delta\varphi\)
849:   depends only on the retarded time, so the readout radius already gives
850:   the value at infinity. The curvature tail is at most
851:   \(M/r_{\rm out}=4.2\times10^{-6}\) (Conjectural).
852: \item
853:   Because \(\alpha=\beta\varphi\) is exactly linear, the second-order
854:   force and source terms are
855:   \(|\delta\varphi|/\varphi_0=1.0\times10^{-27}\) of the first-order
856:   ones.
857: \item
858:   Invariance under \(\varphi\to-\varphi\) makes \(q/\eta\) even in
859:   \(\varphi_\infty\). The readout weight is \(\alpha(\varphi_0)/r\), so
860:   the dynamical part scales as \(\varphi_\infty^2\) (Conjectural for the
861:   \(10^{-8}\) direct part).
862: \item
863:   ADM normalization changes the value by \(6.8\times10^{-11}\)
864:   (Conjectural).
865: \end{itemize}
866: 
867: \textbf{Counterexample candidate.} For the observed photosphere we
868: integrated a spherical gray Eddington envelope with the declared
869: equation of state and Rosseland tables, at fixed mass. It reproduces the
870: declared background to 0.25\% in the charge layers. At the observed
871: \(\log g\) and \(T_{\rm eff}\) it multiplies the endpoint charge by 3.2
872: (1\(\sigma\): 1.6--6.2; 2\(\sigma\): 0.78--11.2). A converged non-gray
873: LTE radiative equilibrium with H and He continuum opacities (flux
874: constancy \(5.5\times10^{-5}\), surface temperature
875: \(0.64\,T_{\rm eff}\)) multiplies it by a further 2.1, to 6.9
876: (1\(\sigma\): 3.9--11.7). The sign is unchanged in every case.
877: 
878: \textbf{Conjectural.} At orbital timescales the memory collapses to
879: static coefficients in every channel we examined.
880: 
881: \begin{itemize}
882: \tightlist
883: \item
884:   Radial. The lowest radial adiabatic mode has period 178 s. A drive
885:   longer than the star exerts a monopole force \(3.3\times10^{-8}\)
886:   times that of the pulse, and \((\omega/\omega_0)^2=1.6\times10^{-6}\)
887:   at the inner orbit.
888: \item
889:   Tidal. Linear response couples the monopole charge only to the \(l=0\)
890:   part of the drive (Proven). The apsidal constant is
891:   \(k_2=3.3\times10^{-4}\), and the l=2 tidal forcing is in the
892:   gravity-wave traveling regime, with a one-way damping depth of
893:   \(8.9\times10^6\) and no discrete resonances, as long as the spin
894:   period exceeds about 45 min.
895: \item
896:   Dissipative. The static structural susceptibility is
897:   \(|\kappa_{\rm struct}|\le8.8\times10^{-9}\), which is
898:   \(2.2\times10^{-9}\) of \(|\beta|\).
899: \end{itemize}
900: 
901: \textbf{Conjectural.} For J0337, a linear, passive, scalar-driven lag of
902: the white-dwarf charge satisfies
903: 
904: \begin{equation}
905: |\delta\Delta|\le|\alpha_o||\kappa_{\rm lag}|\,\delta\varphi_{\rm mod},\qquad \delta\varphi_{\rm mod}\le3.08\times10^{-10}|\alpha_p|,
906: \label{eq:wd-lag-bound}
907: \end{equation}
908: 
909: where the modulation comes from the inner eccentricity. With the Cassini
910: limit \(|\alpha_o|\simeq|\alpha_0|\le3.5\times10^{-3}\)
911: \cite{bertotti2003cassini}, reaching the stored two-day scale
912: \(U=1.68\times10^{-9}\) of Section 5.3 requires
913: \(|\kappa_{\rm lag}\alpha_p|\ge1.5\times10^3\). Even if all of
914: \(|\beta|=4\) relaxed, the bound would be
915: \(4.4\times10^{-12}|\alpha_p|\), and the free-fall mechanism gives
916: \(7.5\times10^{-21}|\alpha_p|\). Within this linear scalar class, a lag
917: at the Section 5 sensitivity must therefore reside in the neutron-star
918: charge sector of Section 4.3.
919: 
920: \textbf{Boundary statement.} This worked example is a no-go boundary,
921: not a loophole. The minimal free-fall internal state exists at
922: millisecond timescales as a transient scattering memory whose permanent
923: value is zero, and at orbital timescales it collapses to static
924: coefficients; assumption A4 holds for it. The remaining assumptions are:
925: 
926: \begin{itemize}
927: \tightlist
928: \item
929:   positive damping of the radial modes;
930: \item
931:   neglect of interior Born scattering (core compactness about
932:   \(10^{-4}\));
933: \item
934:   the unfixed \(\varphi_\infty\) exponent of the \(10^{-8}\) direct
935:   part;
936: \item
937:   a non-gray correction that uses LTE continuum opacity only; non-LTE
938:   heating and line blanketing act in opposite directions;
939: \item
940:   a slowly rotating white dwarf;
941: \item
942:   a mixed hydrogen--helium envelope, whereas the observed star has a
943:   hydrogen (DA) atmosphere \cite{kaplan2014j0337}.
944: \end{itemize}
945: 
